{"activity_text": "Anti-Gram+ & Gram-, Antifungal", "article_id": "", "assay_text": "Bridge", "assay_type": "", "comments_text": "APD analysis reveals that the sequence of THE PEPTIDE shows poor sequence similarity (21.8%) to wasp OdVP3. Activity: Active against Gram- bacteria P. stutzeri, P. fluorescens, A. hydrophila (MIC 0.63-2.5 uM), Gram+ M. luteus, S. aureus, C. glutamicum, M. lysodeikticus (MIC 0.63-5 uM), and yeast P. pastoris GS115 (MIC 2.5-5 uM). Domain activity: PRD is more active than CRD. With the challenge of either Vibrio parahaemolyticus or lipopolysaccharide (LPS), SpHyastatin showed a positive response. The putative NF-kB element acted as a positive regulatory element for this peptide. Knocking down SpHyastatin transcription results in the reduction of crab survival rate when challenged with V. parahaemolyticus ( Shan et al., 2016). Updated 10/2020", "concentration": "", "cytotoxicity_text": "", "hemolytic_activity_text": "", "measure_group": "", "measure_value": "", "pubmed_id": "27493644", "record_granularity": "entry_text", "reference_text": "Front Microbiol. 2016 Jul 21;7:1140. PubMed", "sequence_key": "APD6:AP02723", "source_id": "AP02723", "source_numeric_id": "02723", "source_path": "APD6/apd6_export/structured/peptides.csv", "source_record_id": "AP02723", "source_table": "peptides.csv", "stability_text": "", "subject_name": "", "target_organism_text": "", "title": "The New Antimicrobial Peptide SpHyastatin from the Mud Crab Scylla paramamosain with Multiple Antimicrobial Mechanisms and High Effect on Bacterial Infection.", "unit": "", "﻿database": "APD6"}
